// §6.2(20) with §6.2(11)-(12): a result tied to a named lifetime group
// is no longer-lived than the shortest-lived argument in that group, and
// a prvalue argument is a temporary materialized for the call -- it dies
// at the end of the full-expression.
//
// This rule used to be enforced by accident: resolve_borrow_source_root
// refused a literal as "an arbitrary expression", so the escape was
// rejected without anyone stating why. Once a prvalue correctly
// contributes *no* borrow root -- which §6.2(12) requires, and which is
// what lets `const T&` bind a temporary at all -- the union was just the
// other argument's root and the escape became invisible. The rule is
// stated explicitly now, and this is the case that caught its absence.
import std;

const int& min_ref(
    const int& x [[scpp::lifetime(a)]],
    const int& y [[scpp::lifetime(a)]]
) [[scpp::lifetime(a)]] {
    return x < y ? x : y;
}

int main() {
    int local = 7;
    const int& r = min_ref(3, local);
    return r;
}
